      "dramatis_personae": "- **Squad Col-0 (Dense Amyloplast Statoliths):** Wild-type seedlings packed with heavy, starch-filled amyloplasts that tumble against the endoplasmic reticulum even at minuscule acceleration levels.\n- **Squad PGM1 (The Starchless Floaters):** *pgm1-1* mutants with empty, low-density plastids. They require nearly ten times higher g-forces to trigger the mechanosensitive threshold.\n- **The Perception Sentinels (`PIN3`, `PIN7`):** Auxin efflux carriers positioned at the plasma membrane of columella cells, ready to re-localize when amyloplasts contact the ER meshwork.\n- **The Centrifuge Architects (Dr. John Z. Kiss & Dr. F. Javier Medina):** International research leads directing the EMCS variable rotors from ground control centers in Columbus and ESTEC.\n- **The Gravitropic Relay (`LAZY1`, `LZY2`, `LZY3`):** Key signaling transducers passing the physical contact signal to the lateral root flanks.",
      "script_markdown": "### Act I: Calibration of the Centrifuge (Pages 1–10)\n\n**Page 1**\n- **Panel 1 [Wide Shot / Technical Blueprint]:** The EMCS centrifuge assembly inside Columbus. The dual counter-rotating rotors are spun up to precise angular velocities: `ω = 0.5 rad/s`, dialing in fractional g-levels down to the milligravity regime.\n- **Panel 2:** A cutaway of the Experiment Container (EC). Seeds embedded in dry filter paper are hydrated by remote command from ground control.\n- **Caption (Narrator):** *How much gravity does a plant need to know which way is down? On Earth, 1g is all they have ever felt. In the EMCS centrifuge, we dissect the perception threshold milligravity by milligravity.*\n- **Dialogue (Flight Controller):** \"Hydration valves opened on Rotor A. Temperature nominal at 22.0°C. Centrifuge speed locked to 0.05g.\"\n\n**Page 2**\n- **Panel 1:** Columella cell interior of wild-type Col-0. High-density starch grains (amyloplasts) feel the tiny pull of `0.05g`.\n- **Panel 2:** The amyloplasts sink slowly through the actin filament meshwork, coming into contact with the lower peripheral endoplasmic reticulum.\n- **SFX:** *TING-SHHH (Microscopic sedimentation)*\n- **Dialogue (Dr. Medina):** \"At 0.05g, the amyloplasts are settling! Look at the actin tension vectors—the mechanosensitive ion channels are opening!\"\n\n**Page 3**\n- **Panel 1:** Split screen comparing Col-0 with *pgm1-1* under `0.05g`. In Col-0, the heavy starch grains sediment against the wall; in *pgm1*, the light amyloplasts remain suspended in the cytosol like dust motes in space.\n- **Panel 2:** The Col-0 root initiates a downward curvature angle of 14 degrees. The *pgm1* root continues growing straight forward, blind to the `0.05g` acceleration vector.\n- **Caption:** *Without starch, the weight of the plastid drops below the thermal noise threshold.*\n\n**Page 4–10**\n- Testing the critical threshold ladder: `0.001g` (no response) → `0.01g` (weak transient response) → `0.05g` (definitive biological perception in Col-0).\n- Lunar gravity simulation (`0.16g`): Col-0 displays full, robust gravitropic curvature identical to terrestrial 1g.\n- Martian gravity simulation (`0.38g`): Both Col-0 and *pgm1* display complete downward reorientation, proving that Mars provides more than sufficient gravity for normal plant rooting architecture!\n\n### Act II: The Molecular Gravity Switch (Pages 11–20)\n\n**Page 11–15**\n- Transcriptomic analysis of seedlings exposed to the threshold curve.\n- Immediate-early gene activation within 15 minutes of threshold perception: *TCH2*, *TCH4* (touch/mechanosensitive genes) spike rapidly.\n- *PIN3* and *PIN7* auxin transporters polarize to the lower lateral membranes of the columella, generating the classic Cholodny-Went auxin gradient along the lower root flank.\n\n**Page 16–20**\n- In starchless *pgm1*, the activation threshold is shifted by a factor of 10: it requires `0.3g` before plastid mass overcomes cytosolic resistance.\n- Cell cycle regulation in the root apical meristem: *CYCB1;1* and *CDKB1;1* maintain active division even at `0.01g`, proving that cell division is uncoupled from gravity perception.\n\n### Act III: The Threshold Ledger (Pages 21–32)\n\n**Page 21–25**\n- Synthesis of video tracking data: root tip curvature rate (degrees per hour) plotted against applied g-force.\n- Discovery: The minimum threshold for plant gravity perception is between **`0.01g` and `0.03g`**.\n- Lunar gravity (`0.16g`) and Martian gravity (`0.38g`) are well above the threshold of perception, confirming that lunar and Martian agricultural colonies will not suffer from microgravity root wandering!\n\n**Page 26–30**\n- The engineering takeaway: future space greenhouse centrifuges do not need to generate full 1.0g terrestrial gravity to maintain healthy plant morphology. A gentle `0.2g` rotation is sufficient to establish 100% directional root growth, drastically reducing centrifugal mechanical mass and power consumption.\n\n**Page 31–32**\n- Infographic spread: The Gravitational Threshold Curve and Columella Sedimentation Dynamics.",
