      "dramatis_personae": "- **The Supersonic Steed (Lockheed F-104 Starfighter):** The \"missile with a man in it.\" Climbing vertically from sea level to 26,000 feet, accelerating to Mach 1.6 before pitching over the parabolic hump to create a high-velocity zero-g arc.\n- **The Phosphoprotein Chaperone (`14-3-3κ:GFP`):** Dimeric phosphopeptide reader. Under normal 1g, it rests uniformly in the cytoplasm; upon the sudden impact of 3.5g hypergravity, it rushes to the nuclear envelope, and at the 0g apex, it disperses back into the cytosol.\n- **Squad Starfighter WS (Wassilewskija Ecotype):** 12-day-old petri-dish seedlings strapped into aluminum shock-absorbing flight cassettes inside the avionics bay.\n- **The Rapid Response Kinases (`CDPK1`, `CIPK`, `MAPK6`):** Calcium-dependent protein kinases that fire within 5 seconds of the pilot pulling back the flight stick.\n- **Dr. Anna-Lisa Paul & Dr. Robert J. Ferl:** University of Florida space biologists waiting on the KSC tarmac as the F-104 touches down, ready to flash-freeze the payload tubes in liquid nitrogen.",
      "script_markdown": "### Act I: Runway 15/33 (Pages 1–10)\n\n**Page 1**\n- **Panel 1 [Wide Cinematic Establishing Shot]:** Kennedy Space Center Shuttle Landing Facility (SLF). The three-mile concrete strip shimmers under the Florida sun. A sleek, needle-nosed Lockheed F-104 Starfighter in NASA white-and-blue livery screams down the tarmac, its General Electric J79 afterburner roaring with a 16,000-pound plume of orange flame.\n- **Panel 2 [Cutaway into the Fuselage]:** Behind the pilot's cockpit, in the avionics bay, sits the sealed \"Starfighter Plant Pod.\" Inside, four rows of Petri dishes containing glowing green *14-3-3κ:GFP* seedlings are locked in precision titanium clamps.\n- **Caption (Narrator):** *Spaceflight does not begin at the Kármán line. It begins the instant the afterburner ignites. In the tight confines of a Mach-2 supersonic interceptor, plants experience the most violent gravitational roller coaster on Earth.*\n- **Dialogue (Starfighter Pilot):** \"Starfighter 104 rolling on Runway 15. Gear up. Passing through four hundred knots. Preparing for vertical zoom climb in three... two... one... pulling back!\"\n\n**Page 2**\n- **Panel 1:** The pilot yanks the control stick. The F-104 pitches up into a 70-degree climb. The G-meter needles slam into **`+3.8g`**.\n- **Panel 2:** Subcellular view inside an *Arabidopsis* leaf mesophyll cell. The hypergravity force compresses the vacuole; cytosolic calcium rushes through stretch-activated channels in a microsecond surge: `[Ca²⁺]cyto` leaps 5-fold!\n- **SFX:** *SHKKK-VROOOOM (G-force compression)*\n- **Dialogue (Dr. Paul via telemetry):** \"G-meter reading 3.8g! Channel 1 fixation valve triggered! We've captured the hyper-g state!\"\n\n**Page 3**\n- **Panel 1:** Confocal microscopy visualization. The green fluorescent 14-3-3κ proteins, suddenly mobilized by phosphorylated target proteins, flood from the cytoplasm toward the nucleus, forming an intense bright green ring around the nuclear membrane.\n- **Panel 2:** Inside the nucleus, transcription factors are shielded or sequestered, preventing premature stress transcription.\n- **Caption:** *Within three seconds of hypergravity, the plant's molecular chaperones physically reorganize the cell's command center.*\n\n**Page 4–10**\n- The F-104 climbs through 22,000 feet. The pilot pushes the stick forward into the pushover arc.\n- Suddenly, the G-meter drops from `+3.8g` → `+1.0g` → **`0.01g`**!\n- Complete weightlessness in the cockpit. Loose dust motes float before the pilot's visor.\n- For 30 breathtaking seconds, the Starfighter follows a pure Keplerian parabolic trajectory at 600 knots.\n- Channel 2 fixation fires: injecting RNAlater across the zero-g seedlings at the very apex of the arc.\n\n### Act II: The Fast-Gravity Transcriptome (Pages 11–20)\n\n**Page 11–15**\n- The high-g recovery dive: The jet dives at Mach 1.2, pulling `+4.0g` to level out before returning to base.\n- Wheels touch down on the SLF runway 18 minutes after takeoff.\n- Dr. Ferl and Dr. Paul sprint to the aircraft with cryogenic dewars; the cassettes are extracted within 90 seconds of wheel-stop.\n- RNA extraction and stranded sequencing reveal the \"Fast-Gravity Signature\": genes that respond not to steady-state gravity, but to the **rate of gravitational change (dG/dt)**!\n\n**Page 16–20**\n- Top responders: *TOUCH* genes (*TCH2*, *TCH3*, *TCH4*), heat shock factors (*HSP70*, *HSP90*), and calcium sensors (*CML38*, *CML42*).\n- Crucial discovery: The transcriptional profile of suborbital parabolic flight is fundamentally distinct from long-term ISS orbital microgravity. The plant actively senses the transition shock itself!\n\n### Act III: Suborbital Science for the Commercial Era (Pages 21–32)\n\n**Page 21–25**\n- Comparing Starfighter Flight 1 vs. Flight 2: Reproducibility across supersonic runs.\n- The 14-3-3κ translocation is fully reversible: seedlings that were not fixed return to uniform cytosolic distribution within 10 minutes of landing.\n- Demonstrating that suborbital fighter jets and commercial spaceplanes (Virgin Galactic, Blue Origin) offer viable, low-cost testbeds for space biology hardware before committing to multi-million-dollar orbital missions.\n\n**Page 26–30**\n- The debrief: Engineering biological payloads for commercial spaceflight passengers and crops. How rapid g-transitions during rocket launches prime plants for orbital survival.\n- Closing panel: The F-104 parked against the sunset on the Shuttle Landing Facility runway, while in the KSC lab, fluorescent green root cells glow under the microscope like constellations.\n\n**Page 31–32**\n- Infographic spread: The F-104 Starfighter Flight Envelope and Fast-Gravity Translocation Matrix.",
