      "dramatis_personae": "- **The Weightless Wonder (NASA C-9B Skytrain II):** The legendary padded cabin where generations of Apollo, Shuttle, and ISS astronauts trained for weightlessness.\n- **The Parabolic Vanguard (Squad WS Wild-Type):** Thousands of 14-day-old *Arabidopsis* seedlings experiencing repeated, rhythmic pulses of weightlessness every three minutes.\n- **The Habituation Sentinels (`WRKY33`, `ZAT12`):** Stress transcription factors whose expression spikes violently during Parabola 1, but dampens as the flight progresses toward Parabola 40.\n- **The Antioxidant Shield (`GSTF6`, `PRX34`, `CAT1`):** Glutathione S-transferases and peroxidases neutralizing the microgravity-induced burst of hydrogen peroxide.\n- **The Flight Operators (Dr. Robert Ferl & Dr. Anna-Lisa Paul):** Floating in flight suits inside the padded cabin, gripping handrails as the pilot calls \"Pulling up!\" to \"Over the top!\"",
      "script_markdown": "### Act I: Over the Gulf of Mexico (Pages 1–10)\n\n**Page 1**\n- **Panel 1 [Wide Establishing Shot]:** Interior of the padded C-9 cabin. NASA flight technicians and scientists in royal-blue flight suits float upside-down in zero gravity. Laptop cables and loose tools drift gently. In the center rack, the NASA Parabolic Plant Experiment sits bolted to the aircraft floor.\n- **Panel 2:** Outside the oval cabin windows, the deep turquoise waters of the Gulf of Mexico tilt at a 45-degree angle as the twin-jet C-9 reaches the apex of Parabola 1.\n- **Caption (Narrator):** *Forty times in a single flight, the world turns inside out. One minute you weigh twice your normal mass; the next, you are completely weightless.*\n- **Dialogue (Cockpit Speaker):** \"Approaching apex. Over the top! Thirty seconds of zero-g. Mark!\"\n\n**Page 2**\n- **Panel 1:** Inside the illuminated petri dishes, *Arabidopsis* seedlings float upward against their agar bases. Droplets of condensation peel off the plastic lids and drift into spheres.\n- **Panel 2:** Microscopic view of root epidermal cells. The sudden release from 1.8g to 0.0g triggers an immediate burst of reactive oxygen species (ROS). Fluorescent dye H2DCFDA glows bright neon green!\n- **SFX:** *SHHH-GLOW (Oxidative burst)*\n- **Dialogue (Dr. Paul):** \"First parabola complete! Triggering KFT Bank Alpha! Let's capture the immediate acute response!\"\n\n**Page 3**\n- **Panel 1:** The pilot pulls the nose up for the exit pullout. The G-meter jumps to **`1.85g`**.\n- **Panel 2:** The scientists are slammed into the floor pads. The seedlings are pressed firmly back against the nutrient agar.\n- **Caption:** *The gravitational pulse: not a single shock, but a relentless rhythmic wave.*\n\n**Page 4–10**\n- The cycle repeats:\n  - Parabola 5... Parabola 10... Parabola 20... Parabola 40.\n  - Tracking transcriptional changes across the series:\n    - At Parabola 1: Massive alarm response. Over 1,200 genes spike.\n    - By Parabola 10: General stress genes remain elevated, but mechanosensitive genes begin to stabilize.\n    - By Parabola 40: The plant displays clear **transcriptional habituation**!\n\n### Act II: The Habituation Phenomenon (Pages 11–20)\n\n**Page 11–15**\n- RNA-Seq analysis of Parabola 1 vs. Parabola 40.\n- The Discovery of Biological Habituation:\n  - Immediate-early touch genes (*TCH2*, *TCH3*) show a dramatic reduction in induction magnitude by the 20th parabola.\n  - The plant's mechanoperceptive apparatus recognizes the rhythmic cycle and downregulates its emergency alarms!\n- In contrast, metabolic enzymes and antioxidant defenses (*GSTF6*, *CAT1*) remain persistently elevated, protecting cellular structures from cumulative oxidative stress.\n\n**Page 16–20**\n- The epigenetic mechanism: Transient histone acetylation (*H3K9ac*) opens stress promoters during the first five parabolas, followed by repressive chromatin remodeling that prevents runaway transcription as the sortie continues.\n\n### Act III: The Rhythmic Spaceflight Frontier (Pages 21–32)\n\n**Page 21–25**\n- Comparing Parabolic Aircraft vs. Orbital ISS vs. Supersonic Starfighter:\n  - C-9 Parabolic flight represents a unique dynamic physics environment—oscillatory gravity.\n  - Oscillatory gravity induces higher antioxidant enzyme activity than steady-state orbital microgravity.\n- Application for astronaut crew health: Understanding how biological cells habituate to repeated gravitational transitions during planetary landings and launch aborts.\n\n**Page 26–30**\n- Final debrief: The C-9 lands back at Ellington Field. The scientists haul heavy dewars of liquid nitrogen-frozen cassettes into the waiting laboratory van.\n- Closing panel: An artistic double-page spread showing the parabolic flight arc traced in golden starlight across the blue Gulf of Mexico, with *Arabidopsis* seedlings dancing weightlessly at the crest of every wave.\n\n**Page 31–32**\n- Infographic spread: The 40-Parabola Multi-Omics Habituation Curve and ROS Kinetic Ledger.",
