      "dramatis_personae": "- **Squad Col-0 (The Master Navigators):** Wild-type roots balancing statolith gravitational pull with lateral blue-light attraction.\n- **Squad Phototropin (*phot1 phot2* Mutants):** Blind to directional blue photons; they rely purely on the centrifuge acceleration vector.\n- **Squad Phytochrome (*phyA phyB* Mutants):** Lacking red-light sensors; hypersensitive to blue-light phototropism, exhibiting exaggerated lateral bending.\n- **The Vector Commanders (`PHOT1`, `NPH3`):** Blue-light photoreceptor and non-phototropic hypocotyl 3 adapter protein controlling lateral auxin transport toward light.\n- **The Statolith Vanguard (`PIN3`, `PIN7`):** Graviperception transporters directing auxin toward the acceleration vector.\n- **Dr. John Z. Kiss & Dr. Joshua P. Vandenbrink:** Investigators quantifying the mathematical vector competition between gravitational acceleration and photonic flux.",
      "script_markdown": "### Act I: The Tug-of-War (Pages 1–10)\n\n**Page 1**\n- **Panel 1 [Establishing Shot]:** EMCS centrifuge rotor revolving at lunar speed (`0.16g`). At a 90-degree angle to the centrifugal vector, a bank of blue LEDs (`450 nm`, `20 µmol m⁻² s⁻¹`) illuminates the root tips from the side.\n- **Panel 2:** The root tip of a Col-0 seedling enters the light zone. The statoliths settle toward the outer rim (the gravity vector), while the phototropin receptors on the illuminated flank become autophosphorylated.\n- **Caption (Narrator):** *In weightlessness, light is the only compass. But on the centrifuge rotor, a battle of physical forces begins: gravity pulls outward, light pulls sideways.*\n- **Dialogue (Payload Scientist):** \"Rotor speed 0.16g locked. Blue LED bank activated on Channel B. Let's see which signal the root obeys.\"\n\n**Page 2**\n- **Panel 1:** Cutaway of the root apex. Blue light strikes the right flank; *PHOT1* kinases phosphorylate *NPH3*, triggering asymmetric lateral auxin flow.\n- **Panel 2:** Simultaneously, the `0.16g` centrifugal acceleration pulls the starch-dense amyloplasts toward the bottom wall, activating *PIN3* to drive auxin downward.\n- **SFX:** *ZRRR-KLIK (Signal transduction conflict)*\n- **Dialogue (Bioinformatician):** \"Two opposing auxin fluxes! The columella cell is integrating photonic energy and kinetic acceleration simultaneously!\"\n\n**Page 3**\n- **Panel 1:** Vector diagram overlay across the root tip. Vector **G** (`0.16g` at 180°) plus Vector **L** (Blue photon flux at 90°).\n- **Panel 2:** The resulting root tip orientation resolves at an intermediate angle of **`135 degrees`**—a perfect geometric vector summation!\n- **Caption:** *The plant does not choose one signal over the other. It performs vector calculus at the cellular level.*\n\n**Page 4–10**\n- Testing mutant squads under the vector challenge.\n- In *phot1 phot2*, the root ignores the blue LED entirely, bending 100% downward toward the gravity vector (`180°`).\n- In *pgm1* (starchless), the light vector dominates: without dense statoliths, the root turns 85 degrees toward the blue light, largely ignoring the `0.16g` gravity cue.\n- Discovery: At fractional gravity levels below `0.1g`, phototropism overrides gravitropism; above `0.3g`, gravitropism overrides phototropism!\n\n### Act II: The Signal Transduction Network (Pages 11–20)\n\n**Page 11–15**\n- RNA-Seq analysis of seedlings harvested from the vector competition.\n- Crosstalk between light signaling genes (*HY5*, *COP1*) and auxin response factors (*ARF7*, *ARF19*).\n- Synergistic upregulation of *IAA19* and *IAA14* repressors, preventing runaway bending and stabilizing the resultant angle.\n\n**Page 16–20**\n- Red light modulation: adding background red light via *PHYA* and *PHYB* suppresses blue-light curvature, allowing even weak `0.05g` centrifugal forces to assert dominance.\n- The red-to-blue light ratio acts as a biological tuning knob to control how strongly a crop responds to weak gravitational fields.\n\n### Act III: Designing the Deep-Space Root (Pages 21–32)\n\n**Page 21–25**\n- The comprehensive vector mapping matrix: plotting root angles across 6 gravity levels (`0g`, `0.05g`, `0.1g`, `0.16g`, `0.38g`, `1.0g`) and 4 light intensities.\n- Application for lunar greenhouses: By installing directional blue illumination beneath soil-free hydroponic roots, researchers can amplify rooting anchorage on the Moon (`0.16g`).\n\n**Page 26–30**\n- The astronaut debrief: How crop canopy lighting and subterranean light pipes can ensure that orbital and lunar hydroponic roots never clog drainage channels.\n- Final panel: Astronauts in the lunar habitat greenhouse inspecting crisp, straight rows of lettuce growing along engineered photo-gravitropic vector lines.\n\n**Page 31–32**\n- Infographic spread: The Gravi-Photo Vector Summation Matrix and Transcriptomic Crosstalk.",
