      "dramatis_personae": "- **Squad Irradiated (`gIR`):** Seedlings exposed to a 20 Gray gamma blast, executing full-scale genomic emergency protocols to freeze mitosis and deploy repair complexes.\n- **Squad Mock Baseline (`mock`):** Untreated control seedlings growing peacefully under normal white LED light, maintaining rapid vegetative cell division.\n- **The Mitotic Gatekeepers (`CDKB2;1`, `CYCB1;1`):** Cyclin-dependent kinase complexes required to drive cells into mitosis. Rapidly suppressed in `gIR` to block division of damaged cells.\n- **The DNA Polymerase Vanguard (`POLQ`, `REV1`, `RPA2`):** Translesion DNA polymerases capable of replicating past damaged bases that stall normal replication forks.\n- **The Reactive Oxygen Quenchers (`APX1`, *Ascorbate Peroxidase*):** Enzymes clearing hydrogen peroxide generated by secondary radiolytic ionization of water.\n- **Dr. Lieven De Veylder & Dr. Arp Schnittger:** Genomic researchers decoding the exact threshold of radiation that separates reversible repair from lethal catastrophe.",
      "script_markdown": "### Act I: The Impact of Ionizing Photons (Pages 1–10)\n\n**Page 1**\n- **Panel 1 [Comparative Establishing Shot]:** Two identical growth chambers inside the radiation wing. On the left: Chamber Alpha, dark and shielded, receives a 20 Gray dose of Cobalt-60 gamma rays (`gIR`). On the right: Chamber Beta receives identical mechanical movement without radiation (`mock`).\n- **Panel 2:** High-speed molecular cutaway of root cells in Chamber Alpha. Gamma photons smash through chromatin, breaking hydrogen bonds, detaching purine bases, and leaving jagged double-strand ends.\n- **Caption (Narrator):** *Within milliseconds of irradiation, the cellular landscape is altered. Over twenty thousand genes must decide: continue growing, or freeze everything to save the genome.*\n- **Dialogue (Payload Controller):** \"Radiation pulse complete: twenty Gray delivered. Transitioning to RNA extraction protocol. Let's see what gIR turns on and what it shuts down.\"\n\n**Page 2**\n- **Panel 1:** Heatmap visualization comparing `gIR` vs `mock`.\n- **Panel 2:** A massive column of green (mitotic cell cycle genes) turns pitch black in `gIR`, while a bright crimson column of DNA repair enzymes ignites.\n- **SFX:** *KRRR-STOP (Cell cycle emergency brake)*\n- **Dialogue (Bioinformatician):** \"Mitotic cyclins are plummeting! *CYCB1;1* and *CDKB2;1* have dropped to less than ten percent of mock levels! The G2 checkpoint is locked!\"\n\n**Page 3**\n- **Panel 1:** In the `mock` root meristem, cells divide every 12 hours with regular geometric cell plates forming via *KNOLLE* syntaxin.\n- **Panel 2:** In the `gIR` meristem, cell plate formation stops entirely. The cells swell slightly, channeling all ATP toward double-strand break repair.\n- **Caption:** *The plant willingly sacrifices its growth velocity to preserve genomic fidelity.*\n\n**Page 4–10**\n- Differential expression ledger:\n  - 1,842 genes significantly upregulated in `gIR` (`log2FC > 1.5, FDR < 0.01`).\n  - 1,419 genes significantly downregulated.\n- Upregulation of translesion polymerases: *POLQ* (Polymerase Theta) surges by `+4.8 log2FC`, mediating microhomology-mediated end joining (MMEJ) across difficult chromosome breaks.\n\n### Act II: The Anatomy of the Checkpoint (Pages 11–20)\n\n**Page 11–15**\n- Alternative splicing analysis under ionizing radiation:\n  - Gamma radiation induces extensive intron retention across hundreds of pre-mRNAs.\n  - Splicing factor *U2AF65* is modulated, altering the transcript isoforms of cell cycle kinases to produce non-functional dominant-negative proteins that enforce arrest.\n\n**Page 16–20**\n- Organ-level survival:\n  - The quiescent center (QC) of the root tip acts as the protected stem-cell sanctuary.\n  - QC cells show elevated baseline expression of *SMR5* and *SMR7*, rendering them naturally resistant to radiation-induced programmed cell death!\n\n### Act III: The Planetary Shielding Benchmark (Pages 21–32)\n\n**Page 21–25**\n- Translating the 20 Gy acute dose to deep space chronic radiation:\n  - Surface of Mars: ~0.25 Gy/year (background galactic cosmic rays).\n  - A 20 Gy acute dose equals 80 years of Mars surface exposure delivered in minutes!\n  - Demonstrating that plant DNA repair machinery possesses tremendous latent capacity to buffer cosmic radiation far beyond typical ambient levels.\n\n**Page 26–30**\n- The agricultural takeaway: Crops grown on Mars will not mutate into non-viable dwarfs if their SOG1-MYB3R checkpoint network is intact.\n- Closing panel: Side-by-side comparison of a lush, green *Arabidopsis* rosette that survived the gamma challenge, producing healthy, viable seeds for the next generation of space explorers.\n\n**Page 31–32**\n- Infographic spread: The `gIR` versus `mock` Transcriptomic Volcano Plot and Checkpoint Regulatory Diagram.",
