      "dramatis_personae": "- **The Upright Sentry (`LAZY1`):** The master negative gravitropic promoter. Pulls lateral shoots upward toward vertical alignment (narrow branch angle). In *lazy1* mutants, branches droop downward like weeping willows.\n- **The Canopy Spreader (`TAC1`):** *Tiller Angle Control 1*. The countervailing force that pushes lateral branches outward to maximize light interception. In *tac1* mutants, plants assume an ultra-compact, columnar \"broom\" shape.\n- **Squad Col-0 (The Harmonious Compromise):** Wild-type plants maintaining a golden-ratio branch angle of **45 degrees**, balancing canopy light capture with structural stability.\n- **The Double Mutant (*tac1 lazy1*):** Reversion to intermediate branching, revealing that *TAC1* and *LAZY1* act in direct, antagonistic mechanical opposition.\n- **The Axil Hormone Patrol (`PIN1`, `MAX4`, `YUC`):** Polar auxin transporters and strigolactone enzymes establishing asymmetric auxin accumulation at the adaxial vs abaxial branch boundary.\n- **Dr. Courtney Hollender & Dr. Chris Dardick:** Genetic architects designing optimized crop canopy architectures for high-density spaceflight farming.",
      "script_markdown": "### Act I: The Geometry of the Canopy (Pages 1–10)\n\n**Page 1**\n- **Panel 1 [Wide Establishing Shot]:** Inside an advanced vertical farming rack designed for an orbital transit vehicle. Space is constrained to a 30 cm vertical ceiling between LED shelves.\n- **Panel 2:** Side-by-side comparison of three mature *Arabidopsis* plants: Col-0 with balanced 45° lateral branches; *tac1* with branches pinned tightly against the stem (0°–15° angle); and *lazy1* with lateral shoots spreading horizontally and drooping downward (85°–110° angle).\n- **Caption (Narrator):** *In spaceflight agriculture, volume is the most expensive resource in the solar system. Every millimeter of lateral branch sprawl determines how many plants fit under an orbital LED bank.*\n- **Dialogue (Agronomist):** \"Look at the *tac1* mutants. You can pack four times as many stems per square meter under the LED array without canopy shading!\"\n\n**Page 2**\n- **Panel 1:** Cutaway into the axillary branch junction (the stem-branch hinge). \n- **Panel 2:** Adaxial (upper) vs. abaxial (lower) cell files at the branch base.\n- **SFX:** *KRRR-BEND (Differential turgor pressure)*\n- **Dialogue (Dr. Hollender):** \"Branch angle isn't passive drooping! It's an active, motor-driven Gravitropic Set-point Angle (GSA). *LAZY1* pulls it up; *TAC1* pushes it down!\"\n\n**Page 3**\n- **Panel 1:** In wild-type Col-0, statoliths in the shoot endodermis sense the gravity vector, directing *LAZY1* to pump auxin to the lower side of the branch, generating upward curvature.\n- **Panel 2:** *TAC1* counters this by promoting cell expansion on the upper (adaxial) flank, stabilizing the branch at exactly 45°.\n- **Caption:** *A biochemical tug-of-war that dictates the 3D architecture of every angiosperm on Earth.*\n\n**Page 4–10**\n- Phenotypic dissection of the mutant series:\n  - *tac1-1*: 18° branch angle (columnar, fastigiate architecture).\n  - *lazy1-1*: 88° branch angle (spreading, prostrate, weeping phenotype).\n  - *tac1 lazy1* double mutant: 52° branch angle (restoration of intermediate branching).\n- Discovery: In microgravity, without the gravitropic input to *LAZY1*, *TAC1* dominates—causing lateral shoots in weightlessness to spread wider than on Earth!\n\n### Act II: The Transcriptome of the Axil Hinge (Pages 11–20)\n\n**Page 11–15**\n- RNA-Seq profiling of excised branch junctions.\n- Identification of 842 genes whose expression is strictly correlated with branch angle magnitude.\n- *EXPA1* and *XTH22* are enriched on the adaxial flank in *tac1*, confirming localized cell wall relaxation.\n- Strigolactone signaling (*D14*, *MAX2*) acts upstream of *TAC1* to fine-tune branch outgrowth.\n\n**Page 16–20**\n- Testing under simulated microgravity on a 3D clinostat.\n- Wild-type plants lose their precise 45° angle, drifting outward to 65°.\n- But the *tac1* mutant maintains its tight, upright columnar form even on the clinostat, proving that *tac1* architecture is **gravity-independent**!\n\n### Act III: The Spaceflight Canopy Blueprint (Pages 21–32)\n\n**Page 21–25**\n- Mathematical optimization for spaceflight crops (dwarf tomato, soybean, brassica).\n- CRISPR knockout of *TAC1* homologs in space crops yields high-density \"pillar crops\" that direct all energy toward vertical light capture rather than lateral wandering.\n- Increased harvest index: more biomass per cubic centimeter of growth chamber.\n\n**Page 26–30**\n- The mission debrief: From *Arabidopsis* model genetics to lunar habitat breadbaskets.\n- Final panel: An expansive lunar greenhouse module packed with dense, glowing green pillars of *tac1*-edited space crops under pink LED grow lights, tended by astronaut botanists.\n\n**Page 31–32**\n- Infographic spread: The *TAC1*–*LAZY1* Branch Angle Model and High-Density Spaceflight Canopy Design.",
