      "dramatis_personae": "- **The Endodermal Amyloplast Fleet:** Starch-dense statoliths inside the endodermal sheath cells of the flowering stem. When the stem is tilted horizontally, they tumble downward across the vacuolar membrane.\n- **The Immediate-Early Indicator Brigade (`SAUR19`, `SAUR20`, `SAUR24`):** Small Auxin Up-Regulated RNA genes that ignite within 15 minutes exclusively on the lower stem flank, activating plasma membrane H⁺-ATPases to drive acid growth.\n- **The Proton Pumps (`AHA1`, `AHA2`):** Hydrogen ion motors pumping protons into the apoplast, dropping the cell wall pH from 6.0 to 4.8.\n- **The Acid Wall Looseners (`EXPA1`, `EXPA8`, `XTH4`):** Expansin proteins that disrupt hydrogen bonds between cellulose microfibrils and xyloglucan chains under acidic conditions, allowing turgor pressure to expand the lower flank cells.\n- **The Repressor Feedback Loop (`IAA19`, `IAA29`):** Auxin/IAA transcriptional repressors that surge at 60 minutes to brake the bending motion and prevent overshooting the vertical target.\n- **Dr. Patrick H. Masson & Dr. Chun-Teh Tan:** Space biologists decoding the fastest genetic response to gravity known in the plant kingdom.",
      "script_markdown": "### Act I: The Horizontal Challenge (Pages 1–10)\n\n**Page 1**\n- **Panel 1 [Wide Shot]:** Inside a space biology testing lab, a tray of flowering *Arabidopsis* plants with 10 cm tall flowering stalks (inflorescences) stands upright under green safelight.\n- **Panel 2:** A robotic arm tilts the entire rack 90 degrees to the horizontal position. The clock begins ticking: `T = 00:00:00`.\n- **Caption (Narrator):** *A root takes hours to turn. But a flowering stem must stand proud to disperse its pollen and seeds. Tip it on its side, and within fifteen minutes, its genetic engines roar to life.*\n- **Dialogue (Technician):** \"Tilt confirmed at ninety degrees. High-speed infrared cameras logging at thirty frames per second. Let's watch the endodermal statoliths fall.\"\n\n**Page 2**\n- **Panel 1:** Cutaway into the stem endodermis. Large, globular amyloplasts slide across the cell, striking the lateral plasma membrane within 45 seconds.\n- **Panel 2:** Phosphorylation of *PIN3* transporters occurs instantaneously. Auxin is pumped downward across the stem diameter, flooding into the lower cortical tissue.\n- **SFX:** *FWHHH-BZZT (Rapid bioelectric polarization)*\n- **Dialogue (Dr. Masson):** \"Auxin concentration on the lower flank has doubled in just ten minutes! Look at the *SAUR* genes—they're igniting like wildfire!\"\n\n**Page 3**\n- **Panel 1:** Subcellular view of the lower cortex apoplast. *SAUR19* proteins bind to and activate the *AHA* proton pumps.\n- **Panel 2:** Protons stream into the cell wall matrix. The pH drops: `pH 5.8 → pH 4.9`. The acid growth mechanism is triggered!\n- **Caption:** *The fastest transcriptional response in plant biology: from gravity perception to physical bending in less than a quarter of an hour.*\n\n**Page 4–10**\n- Tracking the upward curve:\n  - 15 min: Microscopic tissue tension emerges; no visible macroscopic bend.\n  - 30 min: The stem tip begins curving upward at 12 degrees.\n  - 60 min: Full upward trajectory at 48 degrees; lower cortical cells are 35% longer than upper flank cells!\n  - 120 min: The stem achieves vertical reorientation (90 degrees upward bend).\n- Laser capture microdissection (LCM) isolates upper vs lower cells, revealing complete transcriptional polarity.\n\n### Act II: The Immediate-Early Gene Ledger (Pages 11–20)\n\n**Page 11–15**\n- RNA-Seq analysis reveals 312 \"Immediate-Early Indicator Genes.\"\n- Top upregulated genes on the lower flank: *SAUR19* (`+6.2 log2FC`), *SAUR20* (`+5.8 log2FC`), *IAA19* (`+4.9 log2FC`), *TCH4* (`+4.1 log2FC`).\n- In contrast, the upper flank shows downregulation or static expression of growth genes, halting upper tissue elongation.\n\n**Page 16–20**\n- Comparison with spaceflight clinostat samples.\n- On a 2D clinostat, the endodermal statoliths roll continuously in circles; the *SAUR* gene burst is completely scrambled, firing symmetrically across the entire circumference.\n- The stem grows in strange, irregular corkscrew shapes because it cannot resolve a clear directional vector!\n\n### Act III: The Planetary Angle (Pages 21–32)\n\n**Page 21–25**\n- The biomechanical engineering perspective: In lunar (`0.16g`) and Martian (`0.38g`) habitats, will flowering crop stalks be able to stand upright against air currents from ventilation fans?\n- Mathematical modeling shows that because the *SAUR* acid growth amplification cascade is non-linear, even the weak `0.16g` of the Moon is sufficient to drive 85% of full terrestrial bending velocity.\n\n**Page 26–30**\n- Final debrief: Inflorescence indicator genes as live biosensors. By attaching luciferase or GFP to the *SAUR19* promoter, astronauts can monitor gravitational stress in real time with handheld luminometers.\n- Closing panel: Golden flowers of *Arabidopsis* waving triumphantly upward inside an orbital station greenhouse, guided by the ancient *SAUR* genetic compass.\n\n**Page 31–32**\n- Infographic spread: The Inflorescence Gravitropism Kinematic Curve and Flank-Specific RNA-Seq Matrix.",
