      "dramatis_personae": "- **The Satellite Capsule (SJ-10 Shijian-10):** The iconic bullet-shaped re-entry capsule designed to conduct orbital experiments in pure zero gravity and return living biological specimens intact to Earth.\n- **The Golden Grain Vanguard (Squad Dwarf Wheat 'Jimai 22'):** A compact, high-harvest-index wheat cultivar engineered to thrive in tight spacecraft chambers, balancing high caloric output with short straw height.\n- **The Photosynthetic Architect (`TaRCA`, *Rubisco Activase*):** The critical enzyme maintaining the catalytic activity of Rubisco under microgravity lighting.\n- **The Starch Synthesizers (`TaSS1`, `TaGBSSI`):** Soluble and granule-bound starch synthases responsible for laying down carbohydrate reserves inside leaf chloroplasts.\n- **The Carbon Re-Routers (`TaSPS`, `TaP5CS`):** Sucrose phosphate synthase and pyrroline-5-carboxylate synthetase diverting carbon from storage into protective osmoprotectant sugars and proline.\n- **Dr. Weiming Cai & Dr. Enkui Duan:** Leading Chinese Academy of Sciences plant biophysicists tracking cereal crop physiological adaptation in autonomous satellite orbit.",
      "script_markdown": "### Act I: The Gobi Ascent (Pages 1–10)\n\n**Page 1**\n- **Panel 1 [Wide Establishing Shot]:** Jiuquan Satellite Launch Center in the Gobi Desert. A Long March 2D rocket roars into the twilight sky, carrying the SJ-10 recoverable satellite into Low Earth Orbit.\n- **Panel 2:** Inside the satellite payload bay, rows of dwarf wheat seedlings in automated growth chambers unfurl their emerald leaves under white-and-red LED arrays.\n- **Caption (Narrator):** *Wheat is the foundation of human civilization. If humanity is to cross the interplanetary void to Mars, wheat must learn to photosynthesize, metabolize, and set grain in weightlessness.*\n- **Dialogue (Launch Director):** \"Orbit insertion confirmed at two hundred and fifty-five kilometers. Microgravity level: ten to the minus five g. Autonomous wheat growth chamber operational.\"\n\n**Page 2**\n- **Panel 1:** Microscopic transmission electron microscopy (TEM) cutaway of a wheat mesophyll cell. Chloroplasts line the plasma membrane.\n- **Panel 2:** In microgravity, the chloroplasts are noticeably more spherical. Thylakoid grana stacks are well-developed, but the starch granules within the stroma are smaller and more fragmented than in ground controls!\n- **SFX:** *ZRRR-HUM (Satellite solar power & LED glow)*\n- **Dialogue (Dr. Cai):** \"Look at the thylakoid membranes—grana stacking is intact! Photosystem II is fully functional in orbit! But the starch granules are shrinking!\"\n\n**Page 3**\n- **Panel 1:** Biochemical pathway schematic. Carbon fixed by Rubisco is not accumulating as complex starch reserves; instead, it is being funneled into soluble sucrose!\n- **Panel 2:** *TaSPS* (Sucrose Phosphate Synthase) gene expression climbs by `+3.4 log2FC`.\n- **Caption:** *The spaceflight wheat plant actively protects itself: soluble sugars act as osmoprotectants, shielding cellular membranes from microgravity stress.*\n\n**Page 4–10**\n- Twelve days of continuous orbital microgravity over the blue continents of Earth.\n- Daily digital camera telemetry records:\n  - Dwarf wheat leaves exhibit slight epinastic downward curvature.\n  - Cell wall thickness in leaf vascular bundles decreases by 14% due to reduced mechanical loading.\n  - Overall photosynthetic rate remains robust at 82% of terrestrial baseline.\n\n### Act II: The Re-Entry Crucible (Pages 11–20)\n\n**Page 11–15**\n- Day 12: Retro-rockets fire. The SJ-10 satellite de-orbits and streaks through Earth's upper atmosphere as a glowing plasma fireball.\n- The parachute deploys over the vast grasslands of Siziwang Banner in Inner Mongolia.\n- Soft touchdown on the steppe. Recovery helicopters land within minutes; the recovery team extracts the plant chamber and rushes the living green wheat seedlings to the mobile laboratory truck!\n\n**Page 16–20**\n- RNA-Seq analysis of the returned satellite wheat:\n  - 1,890 differentially expressed genes.\n  - Major upregulation of carbohydrate transport genes (*TaSUT1*, *TaSUT2*) and proline synthesis (*TaP5CS*).\n  - Downregulation of *TaSS1* (starch synthesis), confirming the biochemical shift from insoluble starch storage to soluble osmotic sugars.\n\n### Act III: The Interplanetary Breadbasket (Pages 21–32)\n\n**Page 21–25**\n- Significance for Mars transit and surface colonies:\n  - Dwarf wheat ('Jimai 22') proves that cereal crops do not suffer catastrophic photosynthetic failure in microgravity.\n  - The elevated sucrose and proline content actually enhances leaf nutritional quality and palatability!\n- Designing planetary vertical farms: Tuning LED light spectra to optimize the *TaSPS* sugar-funneling pathway.\n\n**Page 26–30**\n- The global synthesis: Celebrating international cooperation across space biology missions (NASA, ESA, CAS).\n- Final panel: An astronaut breaking fresh, golden wheat bread inside a Martian habitat dining module, looking out the viewport as dwarf wheat stalks sway under LED canopies on Mars.\n\n**Page 31–32**\n- Infographic spread: The SJ-10 Recoverable Satellite Flight Profile and Wheat Carbon Metabolism Pathway Diagram.",
