      "dramatis_personae": "- **The Space Greens Corps:**\n  - **Mizuna Mustard (*Brassica rapa* var. *japonica*):** Feathery, dark-green serrated leaves packed with peppery glucosinolates and iron.\n  - **Red Romaine (*Lactuca sativa* cv. 'Outredgeous'):** Ruby-red ruffled leaves rich in antioxidant anthocyanins.\n- **The Elemental Bio-Accumulators (The Nutrient Matrix):**\n  - **Potassium (`K`) & Magnesium (`Mg`):** Crucial cardiovascular electrolytes preventing astronaut cardiac arrhythmias in microgravity.\n  - **Iron (`Fe`) & Calcium (`Ca`):** Mitigating spaceflight anemia and bone mineral density loss.\n- **The Antioxidant Vanguard (Anthocyanins, Phenolics, ORAC):** Scavengers neutralizing cellular free radicals generated by cosmic ray exposure.\n- **The Veggie Operations Leads (Dr. Gioia Massa & Matthew Romeyn):** NASA Kennedy Space Center space crop production leads coordinating harvest schedules with ISS crew members.\n- **The ISS Flight Crew (Expedition 59/60 Astronauts):** Performing the pick-and-eat sensory evaluation, drizzling balsamic vinegar and extra-virgin olive oil onto orbital salad feasts!",
      "script_markdown": "### Act I: The Cut-and-Come-Again Harvest (Pages 1–10)\n\n**Page 1**\n- **Panel 1 [Wide Establishing Shot]:** Columbus module of the ISS. The Veggie chamber bellows glow magenta under the dual red-and-blue LED panels. Astronaut Christina Koch floats before the chamber with surgical scissors and sterile wipes.\n- **Panel 2:** She snips five outer leaves from a lush, feathery mizuna plant. Instead of falling, the leaves drift gently before her hands like green feathers in the air.\n- **Caption (Narrator):** *Packaged space rations can keep an astronaut alive, but over years in deep space, essential vitamins break down. Fresh, pick-and-eat salad crops provide what packaged food loses: living antioxidants, vibrant color, and the crisp crunch of home.*\n- **Dialogue (Astronaut Koch):** \"VEG-04B Harvest 2 underway! The mizuna canopy looks incredible! Leaves are deep forest green, super crisp, and smell peppery!\"\n\n**Page 2**\n- **Panel 1:** Close-up of the harvest technique: \"Cut-and-come-again.\" Outer mature leaves are trimmed, leaving the central meristem intact to generate new leaves for future harvests.\n- **Panel 2:** The harvested leaves are wiped with food-safe sanitizing wipes (ProSan) and portioned: half for crew consumption, half frozen in MELFI for ground elemental analysis.\n- **SFX:** *SNIP-CRUNCH (Fresh leaf harvest)*\n- **Dialogue (Payload Scientist Romeyn):** \"Harvest mass logged at two hundred and twenty grams! Save the science samples for the return flight; the rest is cleared for astronaut dinner!\"\n\n**Page 3**\n- **Panel 1:** Inside the ISS galley. Crew members assemble fresh mizuna tacos, laughing as green leaves float into tortillas before being wrapped.\n- **Panel 2:** Sensory evaluation score sheets: Flavor, texture, and aroma all score **8.2 out of 9** on the hedonic scale—higher than Earth-grown control vegetables!\n- **Caption:** *A psychological and nutritional triumph: living food grown in zero gravity.*\n\n**Page 4–10**\n- Return of the frozen science samples to Kennedy Space Center.\n- Analytical chemistry pipeline:\n  - Freeze-drying and homogenization in Teflon ball mills.\n  - Nitric acid microwave digestion for Inductively Coupled Plasma Optical Emission Spectrometry (ICP-OES).\n  - Hydrophilic extraction for Oxygen Radical Absorbance Capacity (ORAC) assays.\n\n### Act II: The Elemental Ledger (Pages 11–20)\n\n**Page 11–15**\n- The ICP-OES elemental results:\n  - **Potassium (`K`):** Exceeds 45,000 ppm (45 mg/g dry mass). Critical for astronaut heart rhythm stability.\n  - **Iron (`Fe`):** Flight mizuna concentrates iron by **`+34%`** compared to ground greenhouse controls (`128 ppm vs 95 ppm`), directly counteracting spaceflight-induced neocytolysis (red blood cell destruction).\n  - **Calcium (`Ca`) and Magnesium (`Mg`):** Elevated by 18% in leaves grown under the Blue-Rich (`50% R : 50% B`) lighting recipe!\n\n**Page 16–20**\n- Total antioxidant capacity:\n  - ORAC assay values average **`82 µmol Trolox equivalents per gram`**.\n  - Anthocyanin pigments in 'Outredgeous' lettuce are 22% higher in orbit due to light-stress photoprotection, creating an enhanced anti-radiation barrier for astronauts who consume them!\n\n### Act III: The Deep Space Diet (Pages 21–32)\n\n**Page 21–25**\n- Nutritional modeling for the 3-year Mars mission:\n  - Calculating daily fresh food supplementation: Just 50 grams of fresh space-grown salad per day provides 100% of daily Vitamin K, 40% of Vitamin C, and over 1,200 ORAC units of cosmic-ray scavenging antioxidants.\n  - Elimination of dietary scurvy and antioxidant depletion on long-duration interplanetary transits.\n\n**Page 26–30**\n- The operational legacy: From Veggie testing on the ISS to automated growth walls on the Lunar Gateway and Mars Base Alpha.\n- Final panel: Astronauts gathered around a dining table inside a Mars habitat, passing bowls of freshly clipped mizuna and lettuce, looking through the panoramic window at the rusty Martian hills.\n\n**Page 31–32**\n- Infographic spread: The Multi-Elemental ICP-OES Ledger and Total Antioxidant Capacity Comparison (Flight vs Ground).",
